INT. TRAFFIC COURT - HEARING ROOM 4 - DAY

Fluorescent glare. Beige carpet. A flag leaning slightly. A bailiff's chair, empty.

JUDGE PERPETUA OSEI-LANGSTROM (60s, robe over a cardigan, reading glasses on a chain) peers down at a file. At the defendant's table, BARTHOLOMEW "BART" KENYATTA-VOSS (40s, wrinkled blazer, a cardboard tube under his arm) stands very straight. Across the aisle, PROSECUTOR ILSE NAKAGAWA-OYELOWO (30s, immaculate, a binder as thick as a phone book) leafs through exhibits.

BART
Your Honor, I'd like to submit the exhibit.

JUDGE PERPETUA
What exhibit, Mr. Kenyatta-Voss?

Bart pulls the cardboard tube open. Out slides a rolled-up poster, which unfurls across the table in a slow, majestic curl: a hand-drawn map of an intersection, annotated in four colors of marker, with arrows, sunbeams, and a smiling cartoon sedan. Ilse gapes. Judge Perpetua removes her glasses, polishes them, puts them back on. Bart steps forward to flatten a corner, knocking Ilse's binder to the floor. She kneels to gather it, he kneels to help, their heads nearly collide, and the poster slides off the table and onto Judge Perpetua's bench, where it covers her gavel.

ILSE
Objection.

JUDGE PERPETUA
On what grounds?

ILSE
The sedan has a face.

JUDGE PERPETUA
Overruled. Mr. Kenyatta-Voss, proceed.

BART
Your Honor, as the map shows, the stop sign was obscured by a sycamore.

ILSE
It was obscured by a sycamore he planted.

BART
Prove it.

ILSE
I have the permit.

She holds up a stapled paper. Bart looks at it. Looks at the poster. Looks at the cartoon sedan, which smiles back.

BART
(quietly)
I'll pay the fine.

JUDGE PERPETUA
Forty dollars, payable at the window.

She raps the gavel through the poster. It makes a small soft tearing sound.
